# Keep the build context small, deterministic, and — critically — FREE OF ENV.
#
# `.env*` is the load-bearing line. `client/.env` exists on the dev machine with
# NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8080, and Next inlines NEXT_PUBLIC_*
# into the JS bundle AT BUILD TIME. If that file reached the build context, the
# production bundle would ship hard-coded localhost and every API call from a
# real browser would fail. It is gitignored, so a build from a clean clone never
# sees it — but a build from the working directory would. This line is what makes
# the two cases identical.
#
# Leaving the vars UNSET is deliberate, not an oversight: axios then issues
# relative URLs and socket.io-client falls back to the page origin, so the image
# never learns its own hostname and the Vercel cutover is a DNS + ingress change
# rather than a rebuild. The Dockerfile asserts this held.
#
# There is a SECOND leak path, found while checking what output:'standalone'
# does on Vercel: writeStandaloneDirectory() copies .env and .env.production
# into .next/standalone, where the runtime server.js reads them at START-UP.
# So an .env in the context would not only inline NEXT_PUBLIC_* at build time,
# it would ship into the image and be re-read at runtime, outranking whatever
# compose passes in. One line closes both.
.env
.env.*

node_modules
.next
out
build
.git
.vercel
*.tsbuildinfo
*.log
npm-debug.log*
.DS_Store
